        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deep_Run%2C_North_Carolina"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Before becoming Deep Run, the settlement was nicknamed Red Town because many homes sported bright red tin roofs.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deep_Run%2C_North_Carolina"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The town’s charter was revoked in the 1970s when it could no longer meet North Carolina’s municipal standards.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deep_Run%2C_North_Carolina"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Today Deep Run remains a quiet agricultural hub, hosting a handful of small businesses and light industry.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deep_Run%2C_North_Carolina"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The community still maintains its own fire service, water and sewer systems, a bank, and a postal route.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deep_Run%2C_North_Carolina"
    },
    {
